WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:03.840
- Me coming from Africa, I
came here to better my life.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.480
And I feel like personally we need to stop

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.930
using the word racism when
we don't get something

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.153
or when something don't go our way

00:00:12.153 --> 00:00:14.070
and just keep trying.
- Let me jump in real quick.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.960
No disrespect, but racism exists

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.110
and I have to acknowledge it
and I can't just ignore it,

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.987
but what I can do is
not buy into the system.

00:00:21.987 --> 00:00:24.570
(tense music)

00:00:26.491 --> 00:00:29.074
(gentle music)

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:34.830
- [Interviewer] Step forward if you agree.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.113
Slavery affected my family.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.220
- Yeah. I mean that's just
evident in my last name King.

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.740
I mean that's the brand of the slave owner

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.630
that put that name on my
family all those years ago

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:53.640
and I'm still wearing it in those ways

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.240
along with the compounded,
you know, economic disparity,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.400
social disparity, mental
trauma, all of that.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.790
I mean, it's tethered forever.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.440
- So to go back to slavery

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:09.163
and talk about post-traumatic
stress disorder,

00:01:09.163 --> 00:01:10.920
it has never stopped.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.360
So there is no post.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.890
What my great-grandparents
were teaching my grandparents

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.750
who taught my mother who taught me

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:21.330
came from what they
learned through slavery.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.210
So there's a lot of fear,
a lot of insecurity,

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.700
a fear of society, a
fear of law enforcement.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.580
So slavery has made a major impact

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.720
where the low self-esteem
is consistently reoccurring.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.110
I think for me growing up
in Los Angeles, California,

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.840
in the early 1960s,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.060
there was a stereotype that
I didn't want to be Black.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.050
I didn't wanna be African

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.930
because we come from
Africa or we're slaves.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:47.250
So I want to be a Chinese boy.

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.990
I want to be Bruce Lee.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:50.670
So growing up like that,

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.710
I didn't like myself and who I was.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.470
I didn't like the color of my
skin or the way that I spoke.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:57.000
So I emulated other people.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.520
I want to be something that I was not.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:00.480
- It's very saddening

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:04.320
when you can literally only
go back a couple generations

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.170
and then it's like the
documents just don't exist.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:08.100
There's no record.

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.480
It's like, you know, my family
has completely been erased.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.430
In that same point too, in
the time that we've been here,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.950
we've created an incredible culture

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.540
and we have so many things to be proud of

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.340
and I always reframe the narrative myself

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.190
and I speak of my ancestors
that were enslaved

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.230
as you know doctors and healers

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:26.640
and just like, you know, we were taken

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:27.900
because we were talented.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:28.733
- Right.
- You know.

00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:30.630
- Because we had skills to offer

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.790
and we built this country, so.

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:34.050
- And still building.

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:34.883
- And still building.

00:02:34.883 --> 00:02:36.716
That's something to take pride in, so.

00:02:36.716 --> 00:02:38.633
- [Interviewer] Can the
disagreers step forward?

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.110
- Slavery didn't affect my family,

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:46.260
but we did suffer a different
type of slavery back home.

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:48.240
Like we went to colonization.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.950
Like I am Bamileke.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.750
There was a massacre of Bamileke.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:52.830
You guys can look it up.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:56.760
They kill like more than
300,000 of my people

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.550
because we didn't want to assimilate

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.710
to the French culture,

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.180
but it's still nothing compared

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:07.260
to what you guys' ancestor
like been through.

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.093
You know.

00:03:08.093 --> 00:03:09.810
- I wouldn't wanna
minimize that pain though.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.250
What you just said hurt me.

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.890
That 300,000 people were killed

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:15.330
because they would not conform

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.700
to the colonization of the French people.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.190
So that is just as painful and hurtful

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.160
as the the trauma that we
suffered here in America.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.500
- I can't share the same experience

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.900
that you have went
through and your family,

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:30.930
even though we also had
our own type of slavery.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:32.550
We had Apartheid at the time,

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:35.010
you know, where the Dutch people took over

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.440
and they wanted to just have South Africa

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.660
and maybe and I think other countries

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.370
where we could only just speak Dutch.

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:42.930
It's somehow still happening.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.810
Even now there's like
a place in South Africa

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:48.180
where it's called Orania, you know,

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.890
and it's just Dutch people there.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.080
No Black person is even allowed there.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.410
So we still somehow feel
that oppression even now,

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:59.850
but it's nothing compared to
what your ancestral history,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:01.080
you know, has gone through.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:04.500
I'm somehow happy that
you guys have forgiven it

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.330
even though you haven't forgotten it.

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:07.163
Have you forgiven it?

00:04:07.163 --> 00:04:10.290
I mean that face is like.
(everyone laughs)

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:13.200
- But me personally, no I haven't,

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.670
but I wanna work through the change.

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:16.470
But no, I have not forgiven

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:17.640
because it's a system
- And it's understandable.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:18.767
that's still going on.

00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:20.549
And from what I'm hearing from you guys,

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:24.180
there's a system that's still
going on in Africa as well

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.240
and it's a war on us.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.730
- [Interviewer] Racism is
the main cause of poverty

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.653
among Black people in America.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:40.860
- Racism is a very big issue

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:44.670
that talking about it I'm
really trying to be careful

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:48.960
to not like say anything
that will be insensitive.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.360
If opportunity is right here,

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.610
they will give it to people of other race

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.340
than African American and Africans too.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.970
So if there's a job that
maybe have math requirement,

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:02.527
I feel like they'll pick an
Asian person or they believe,

00:05:02.527 --> 00:05:04.710
"Oh, African Americans will
not dress appropriate",

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.420
so they will pick another person.

00:05:06.420 --> 00:05:10.470
They will always like pick
people that are not Black

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:11.970
over people that are Black.

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:12.900
- And I also think,

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.640
I think the crucial like missing point

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.160
when we talk about economic disparity,

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.620
time, time is the most valuable currency.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:21.450
And if you have like a stock,

00:05:21.450 --> 00:05:23.250
that's like the American
dream is the stock

00:05:23.250 --> 00:05:25.710
and you know during all
that time while that asset

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:27.660
the American dream was appreciating

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.370
and you're not allowed to
take out business loans,

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.020
you're not allowed to read,
you're not allowed to write.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:33.240
You have to first fight to
become three fifths of a citizen.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.610
Then you got Jim Crow and
then you got everything else.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:39.150
It's like you're now having
to buy in at a price so high

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.340
because the time is irreparable.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.870
You can't get that back.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:46.680
One Black person today cannot
in one lifetime achieve

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.140
what generations of white
families were able to do

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.300
simply because they didn't have
to compete with us to do it.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:52.320
- Yeah.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.150
I agree with you totally like.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:54.983
- Yeah

00:05:54.983 --> 00:05:56.340
I was thinking just like you said

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:58.020
from a historical standpoint,

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.910
we start with slavery.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:03.090
Then that's centuries,
generations of stolen labor

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:03.923
like you said.

00:06:03.923 --> 00:06:04.860
Then we get into Jim Crow,

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:06.810
which is just an extension of that.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:07.830
Then we have redlining.

00:06:07.830 --> 00:06:09.210
We have mass incarceration.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:12.120
We have events like the bombing of Tulsa

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:13.230
and Rosewood in Florida

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.340
and all these different examples

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.840
of our wealth being decimated.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:17.580
And I don't say that to
come from a defeatist

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.380
or a victim mindset,

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:21.180
but I think if we just
have an honest conversation

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.340
about history and what we've gone through

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:25.170
when we've been here,

00:06:25.170 --> 00:06:26.970
racism is definitely a big part of that.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:29.850
And there are a lot of
successful Black Americans,

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.340
a lot of millionaires, a lot
of you know business owners,

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:35.340
but they're exceptions
for sure for a reason.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:37.920
And there's a big difference
between income and wealth.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.780
I would like to challenge the ideal

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:41.670
that African Americans are lazy

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.100
or that we don't have a good work ethic.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:46.620
I think there's this perception
that, you know, we're here.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:47.453
We're in America,

00:06:47.453 --> 00:06:49.670
How are we not doing
better for ourselves, so?

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.010
- I thought I was gonna
be the only one staying.

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.140
I was gonna be like, "Oh I'm
about to be like Kanye West."

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:02.250
But okay, there's a lot of
thing we can use as an excuse

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:05.850
to hold us back to not
to reach our potential,

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:09.180
but it's up to us to look past that.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:11.160
You know, like me coming from Africa,

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:14.130
I came here to better my life, my family.

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:15.660
I'm the only one here.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:16.770
And since I have been here,

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:18.390
there's a lot of bad
things that happened to me.

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:20.760
There's also a lot of good
things that happened to me.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:23.070
As African or African American,

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:25.650
we need to like look past like racism

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:27.150
and like just focus on our own goal.

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:28.650
Like what do we want to achieve?

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:32.520
And we need to stop using the word racism

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.200
when we don't get something

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.210
or when something don't go our way

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:38.070
and just keep trying, you know.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:39.720
- Let me jump in real quick.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.610
No disrespect, but racism exists

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:44.760
and I have to acknowledge it
and I can't just ignore it,

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.220
but what I can do is
not buy into the system.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:48.390
You know, if I want to do something,

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:49.740
let me be the entrepreneur.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:52.200
What I believe happened in the 1960s

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.410
is that we started
looking for equal rights,

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:57.060
to be equal with other people.

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.520
And what we stopped doing, and
I heard you talk about Tulsa,

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.220
we stopped using the
Black-owned businesses.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.860
Now, because you guys gotta understand

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.830
the Fair Housing Act in 1968,

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:09.180
I'm five years old.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:11.880
That's when we could move
off the east side of LA

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:13.890
and move into other subcultures.

00:08:13.890 --> 00:08:17.130
So what I wanted to do at
eight years old was go to IHOP.

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:19.620
I didn't want to go to the
ma and pa restaurant anymore.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.650
I want to go to let's
go eat at the hotels.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:25.410
So what we did is stopped
supporting each other

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:27.660
and started supporting corporate America.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.790
So the racism plays a major part,

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:31.470
but we don't have to buy into it.

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.683
- I have been waiting to
disagree with you, my brother.

00:08:35.625 --> 00:08:38.130
I'm waiting patiently.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:41.220
The thing is, even though I
want to disagree with you,

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:42.750
I get where you're coming from.

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.450
When I see Black people
talking about racism,

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:46.533
all that stuff,

00:08:46.533 --> 00:08:47.880
I'll be like, "What is
wrong with these people?

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:48.990
You guys have food.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:51.210
You have EBT, all this thing

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:54.090
and you are here shouting
about somebody's racist

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:56.340
to just leave that person and move on."

00:08:56.340 --> 00:08:57.180
I feel that way.

00:08:57.180 --> 00:08:59.910
Like why are they always like complaining?

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:02.700
But when Black Life
Matters started happening,

00:09:02.700 --> 00:09:04.297
like I sat down, I said,

00:09:04.297 --> 00:09:07.860
"I need to actually know why
these people are pained."

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:10.140
And if we look at the history,

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.130
what they've done to them.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:12.360
Like if you actually sit down,

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:13.710
watch some documentaries,

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:15.360
how they were I maltreated.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:16.680
I don't think if I'm actually born there,

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:17.850
I will forgive those people.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:18.683
I don't care.

00:09:18.683 --> 00:09:21.060
It's easier for us Africans
that were raised in Africa

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:22.230
to feel like they're complaining

00:09:22.230 --> 00:09:24.570
because we were raised through struggle.

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:26.400
Like I barely even have water.

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:27.960
And you, you have water,
you are complaining.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.990
But then it's not about
the basic necessity

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.000
or about how we are raised.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.870
We are raised like, I don't
want to say way better.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:39.270
We might not have resources,
but there's some way,

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:42.390
like our own people
will not look down on us

00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:43.410
or put us behind.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.480
So it's in their nurture that way.

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:46.950
Like they just can't overlook it.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:49.020
And if you sit down and watch everything

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:52.290
that the forefathers have
been through for you,

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.310
I change my mind.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:54.690
No, they need to be angry.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:56.837
However, they should still move forward.

00:09:56.837 --> 00:09:59.760
- [Prosper] I mean, we have
police brutality back home too.

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:00.593
- True.

00:10:00.593 --> 00:10:02.070
- We have, like if you go to Cameroon

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:04.830
and then you see how the
police officer like treat us

00:10:04.830 --> 00:10:06.780
and they're Black just like us,

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:08.310
you're not even gonna believe it.

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:09.143
You know?

00:10:09.143 --> 00:10:10.789
And in Cameroon we have this,

00:10:10.789 --> 00:10:13.428
have you guys heard the
word like tribalism?

00:10:13.428 --> 00:10:14.580
- Yeah, yeah

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:15.413
- It happens in Nigeria too.

00:10:15.413 --> 00:10:16.246
- Yeah.

00:10:16.246 --> 00:10:17.853
So we do get oppressed too.

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:19.553
- [Elizabeth] I agree.

00:10:19.553 --> 00:10:22.290
- So when I'm saying like we
need to like look past that,

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.720
I'm not saying forget what
happened to you guys or me.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:25.830
At the end of the day,

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:29.250
we cannot just sit and
just grieve on the past.

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:32.040
We have to be able to
move on, but not forget.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:33.930
If I could choose a place to live,

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:35.490
it will obviously be my country.

00:10:35.490 --> 00:10:36.510
I love my country,

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:40.770
but right now my country is
not the best choice for me.

00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:43.500
There's a little genocide
going on in Cameroon right now.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:46.380
So many innocent life have been taken.

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:47.970
If I was still in my country,

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:49.860
the opportunity that I have here

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:52.500
I would not have those opportunity there.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:54.960
- [Interviewer] Hip-hop
reinforces stereotypes

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:56.343
about the Black community?

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.270
- In South Africa we have
our own version of hip hop,

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:07.920
so we call it (speaking foreign language).

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.290
So they would rap in our
own language, in different,

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:11.250
we call it (speaking foreign language).

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:16.080
So back home it kind of did
reinforce some stereotypes

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:18.210
because now when you go out in the world,

00:11:18.210 --> 00:11:21.630
people will just classify
you under one group.

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:24.930
And growing up also looking
at American entertainment,

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:26.910
looking at the hip-hop that's done here,

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:28.860
I'm like, "Oh well, you're
rapping about drugs.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:29.790
You're rapping about this."

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:30.960
Of course, it's gonna happen.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.040
Of course, you're gonna go to jail.

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:35.280
But getting older you see the
culture as well of hip-hop.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:39.060
I had to understand that
there's a culture within that.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:41.100
- I was alive when hip-hop started

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.830
and it was during a time

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:46.770
where people rapped about what they saw,

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.530
what they wanted, what
they wanted in life.

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:51.450
And then it became where people rapped

00:11:51.450 --> 00:11:53.940
about what they had been through,

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:56.880
what they had gotten over,
you know, used to sell drugs,

00:11:56.880 --> 00:11:59.790
used to hurt people, but
now this is who they are.

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:04.790
And I think it's gotten into
now, in our day and age now,

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:07.290
it's where people are glorifying

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:09.870
and wanting to pretend
that these are the things

00:12:09.870 --> 00:12:12.900
that they do on a daily
basis when it's not.

00:12:12.900 --> 00:12:14.850
So it went from storytelling

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:17.340
to now really it's truly storytelling,

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:19.950
but it's unhealthy for our culture.

00:12:19.950 --> 00:12:21.180
- I don't want to turn on my radio

00:12:21.180 --> 00:12:23.130
and all I'm hearing is
I put a bullet in it,

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:25.170
something, something, like I'm like, "Eh?

00:12:25.170 --> 00:12:26.700
That's what you could do."

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:27.960
No, like.

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:32.280
And then it's all about like
talking about female body type,

00:12:32.280 --> 00:12:33.480
the booty, the tits.

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:35.310
Like don't talk about my booty like that.

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:38.490
So I don't want to listen
to something that portray me

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:39.870
as a female that way

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.010
or something that is talking about

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:42.270
how you put bullets in my head.

00:12:42.270 --> 00:12:44.370
Like if you actually
do not come to America

00:12:44.370 --> 00:12:45.690
and see for yourself,

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:48.420
you will think that most African Americans

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:51.120
are carrying guns around
and they just shoot randomly

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:53.070
or they just grab your (beep)
or something like that.

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:54.750
That's the way the music portray them.

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:58.140
And music is a very important
element in our society

00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:01.800
'cause it influence a lot of
people, both youngs and adult.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:04.170
And then they kind of see all those stuff

00:13:04.170 --> 00:13:06.150
and they act like that

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:07.980
even though that's not what
they really want to be.

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:09.270
They act like that and behave like that.

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:13.020
Like if all these rappers
are your role model,

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:14.250
then you wanna be like them.

00:13:14.250 --> 00:13:15.300
You wanna be cool too.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:16.740
So in the end,

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:19.320
it's about like the people
that are packaging the music

00:13:19.320 --> 00:13:23.790
and sometimes about trend
is what people want to hear.

00:13:23.790 --> 00:13:26.790
- [Interviewer] The American
dream is only for white people.

00:13:31.800 --> 00:13:34.590
- I believe that the American
dream is only for white people

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:36.990
because it says equal.

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:38.160
And when we talk about equal,

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:42.360
there's no equality between the 1%,

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.580
the Rothschilds, the
Rockefellers, the Trumps,

00:13:44.580 --> 00:13:47.580
and the people who really run the media

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:48.780
and things of that nature.

00:13:48.780 --> 00:13:51.240
So for Black people growing up in America

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:52.530
and living in America,

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:54.960
we could become rich,
we could become wealthy,

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:58.980
but we are not on the equal
playing field of white America.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:01.830
The system of white supremacy

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:03.360
which is in the law enforcement,

00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:05.700
which is in the judicial system,

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:09.570
which is in the prison
industrial association,

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:12.090
also in the politics,

00:14:12.090 --> 00:14:15.213
we are not equal in any of those terms.

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:22.199
- When I was back there
and when you were speaking,

00:14:22.199 --> 00:14:26.040
I started thinking like,
"Could we ever actually

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:29.520
as Black people have the
American dream here in America?"

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:34.470
And I don't know if we really can being,

00:14:34.470 --> 00:14:35.790
I'm trying to be careful with my words,

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:40.500
but I do think there needs
to be a degree of separatism,

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:42.900
like Black people we
really need to have our own

00:14:44.280 --> 00:14:46.710
to fully take part in that dream.

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:48.120
- [Skipp] This is their system.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:48.953
- Yeah.

00:14:48.953 --> 00:14:52.200
- Why would we dream of
being equal in a system

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.003
that wasn't designed for us?

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:00.540
- The American dream, at least
the way that I define it,

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:03.090
is you know, freedom of opportunity,

00:15:03.090 --> 00:15:06.810
freedom to choose what
I what I want to do,

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:08.880
freedom to acquire wealth the same way

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:10.440
that anybody else can.

00:15:10.440 --> 00:15:13.710
Obviously, we're fighting
the tides of time on that

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:15.600
in our circumstances,

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.880
but it's hard because
it's hard to honor that,

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:19.650
but then also want more for myself.

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:20.700
And I think that as Black people,

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:23.377
we're always teeter-tottering
on that line of like,

00:15:23.377 --> 00:15:24.330
"Well, am I American?

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:25.650
Like do I get the American dream

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.870
or do I, do we just tear
all this (beep) down

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:28.710
and build something new?"

00:15:28.710 --> 00:15:30.180
White people are not
the only people entitled

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:32.190
to the American dream ever.

00:15:32.190 --> 00:15:33.023
- [Lethabo] Yeah.

00:15:33.023 --> 00:15:34.350
- And I think that
that's why we're all here

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:36.960
because our ancestors made it so

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:39.420
that we can stand here and
pursue that American dream.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:40.590
- I wouldn't have been here

00:15:40.590 --> 00:15:43.230
if it meant that it was not for everyone.

00:15:43.230 --> 00:15:45.420
I wouldn't have even entered America.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:47.280
You know, I feel like not just African,

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:49.920
but other people as well
in different countries.

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:51.870
And from what I have seen,

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:54.840
I've seen other people, even Africans

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:58.230
and African Americans
succeeding in their own way.

00:15:58.230 --> 00:15:59.340
And that's why I feel like,

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:02.010
like you said you can
interpret the American dream

00:16:02.010 --> 00:16:03.330
in a different way,

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:06.810
whatever industry or whatever
it is that you're focusing on

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:09.870
because there is a level
of opportunity there.

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:12.180
There is some way that
you can be successful.

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:13.950
You know, talking about
Trevor Noah, you know,

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:15.630
who took over "The Daily Show".

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:16.860
It wouldn't have been possible for him

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:18.060
coming in as an African.

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:19.800
He got his American dream.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:20.940
It made us as well,

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:23.520
other Africans believe
that it is possible.

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:26.550
- The American dream writes his check,

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:29.790
so he gets to have wealth

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:31.950
or whatever it is that he's experiencing,

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:33.690
but the American dream is the one

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:35.673
who writes his check, not him.

00:16:36.525 --> 00:16:37.435
- Hmm.

00:16:37.435 --> 00:16:38.400
- I agree with you.

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.257
I hear you.
(everyone laughs)

00:16:40.257 --> 00:16:41.670
But do you see how
- But you can

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:42.530
- we would put it
- reach for the stars.

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:44.100
as he had his American dream

00:16:44.100 --> 00:16:45.183
even though someone writes his check.

00:16:45.183 --> 00:16:47.310
- I understand.
- But for him

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:49.560
or for some of us looking
at it on the other side,

00:16:49.560 --> 00:16:53.220
that's his American dream because
his goal was to get there.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:56.340
The biggest cultural
shock I saw coming here

00:16:56.340 --> 00:16:58.200
was definitely the lifestyle

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:00.840
because the lifestyle I saw of America,

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:02.310
especially Los Angeles,

00:17:02.310 --> 00:17:05.130
was portrayed as a very rich lifestyle.

00:17:05.130 --> 00:17:07.440
And of course I came here as a student

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:10.470
and coming here, living here
and seeing the reality of it,

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:13.800
seeing different homeless
people, disadvantaged people,

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:15.540
was really a cultural shock for me

00:17:15.540 --> 00:17:17.340
because I didn't expect to see that.

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:21.240
And I guess also the way
people were handling each other

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:23.280
because coming in as an African

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:24.810
I thought people were together.

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:26.460
But since being here,

00:17:26.460 --> 00:17:28.380
I saw that there was a huge divide

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:29.880
or there is still a huge divide

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:32.940
within the African American
community themselves.

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:34.890
- If he had stayed in South Africa,

00:17:34.890 --> 00:17:38.893
would he have had the same
opportunity that he had here?

00:17:38.893 --> 00:17:39.726
- No.
- No.

00:17:39.726 --> 00:17:42.900
- Like, you know, so corporate
is writing his check.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:43.860
But like us growing up,

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:45.840
the reason we move here is

00:17:45.840 --> 00:17:47.730
because we don't have it better back home.

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:49.800
But like for me,

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:52.380
I'm the first person ever
in my family in America.

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:54.180
Everybody else is back home.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:56.010
Everybody's depending on me.

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:59.280
All of them, my mom,
she's dying to come here.

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:02.640
Like she willing to do
anything just for her

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:03.780
to be in America.

00:18:03.780 --> 00:18:06.780
So for me, when I look at
those like little things,

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:09.420
I'm like, "Yeah, American
dream is for everybody".

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:10.253
You know.

00:18:10.253 --> 00:18:11.960
Like, yeah, we might not
have the same opportunity

00:18:11.960 --> 00:18:13.320
as do white people,

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:15.330
but there's still that little chance

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:16.670
that a lot of us back home

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:18.873
are willing to take just to get here.

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:22.527
- [Interviewer] I trust law enforcement.

00:18:22.527 --> 00:18:24.872
(everyone laughs)

00:18:24.872 --> 00:18:25.783
- I don't have a thing to say.

00:18:25.783 --> 00:18:28.733
- [Interviewer] All right, can
the disagreers step forward?

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:39.360
- I got married to a young lady

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:43.140
who did 15 years in prison for
a robbery she didn't commit.

00:18:43.140 --> 00:18:47.430
I myself was incarcerated for six months

00:18:47.430 --> 00:18:50.130
for a burglary that I
couldn't have committed

00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:51.750
because I was in prison.

00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:55.500
So in 1987, I'm doing 19 months in prison.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:59.280
When I came home in 1988, I
was given a burglary charge

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:00.660
and I had to sit six months

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:03.240
and the judge did not kick it out

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:05.280
because there's no way
I could have done it.

00:19:05.280 --> 00:19:08.220
He kicked it out because they
didn't serve me the warrant

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:09.320
while I was in prison.

00:19:10.290 --> 00:19:11.340
So I don't trust them.

00:19:11.340 --> 00:19:12.173
They lying.

00:19:12.173 --> 00:19:14.280
They do things and it affects our people.

00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:17.100
So many people I know who
have been wrongfully arrested,

00:19:17.100 --> 00:19:20.070
wrongfully convicted, and they
actually lie on the stand.

00:19:20.070 --> 00:19:21.960
So there's no trust.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.030
- As you guys know, I am
from Richmond, Virginia.

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:26.460
It was the capital of the Confederate.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:29.700
I have been brutally
abused by the police there,

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:30.600
like personally,

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:33.120
I was at the river on the 4th of July.

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:34.050
I don't smoke.

00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:35.100
I don't do none of that.

00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:36.180
The police come and arrest me

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:38.220
because I look like a drug dealer.

00:19:38.220 --> 00:19:39.750
Like they like arrested me.
- Wow.

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:40.583
- What?

00:19:40.583 --> 00:19:41.416
The stereotype!

00:19:41.416 --> 00:19:44.310
- Then when I was in high
school my senior year,

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:47.610
my father went to jail over a
car accident, a car accident.

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:50.130
And I was left alone at 17 years old.

00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:51.330
I was grateful.

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:55.890
One of my teacher took
me in and like raised me.

00:19:55.890 --> 00:19:58.950
I try to stay away from the
police as much as possible.

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:00.150
- First of all, they're human beings

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.069
and I think that just
because you put a badge on

00:20:02.069 --> 00:20:06.210
that doesn't magically bewitch
you with like integrity

00:20:06.210 --> 00:20:08.340
and a desire to actually
help the people around you.

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:09.856
I think a lot of times police culture

00:20:09.856 --> 00:20:11.880
comes from police families.

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:13.770
It comes from like legacy lines

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:15.600
and this whole like kind of
like culture about stuff.

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:17.670
I mean, I don't trust them anymore

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:19.740
than I would trust any other person.

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:21.720
They haven't earned that necessarily,

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:23.460
especially not as an institution.

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:25.530
In its purest form, I
think it's necessary,

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:28.980
but they have a lot of
living up to that to do

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:32.400
and to make up for if
they're gonna be trusted.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:34.320
- 'Cause I've seen law
enforcement here on social media

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:38.010
and seeing how they treat
people of color, you know,

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:39.180
from like hitting them.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:41.250
I mean George Floyd,
you know, rest in peace.

00:20:41.250 --> 00:20:44.670
But just seeing that from
my experience being here,

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.350
I was scared, you know.

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:47.697
Like every time I'd see a cop car pass by,

00:20:47.697 --> 00:20:49.830
I'm like, "Okay, I better
make sure I'm driving okay,"

00:20:49.830 --> 00:20:51.390
you know, 'cause I don't wanna be stopped

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:53.940
or make sure you're not in
any harm's way, you know.

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:55.470
But it's sad to feel that,

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:58.050
to feel that pressure every
time you see a cop going by

00:20:58.050 --> 00:20:59.880
and even if they trying to help,

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:01.320
but I wouldn't even want that help

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:04.500
because of what I've been seeing
on social media, you know.

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:07.050
And also seeing the case
of people being wrongfully,

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:09.330
you know, taken to prison.

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:10.163
That's like unfair.

00:21:10.163 --> 00:21:11.820
Like what type of research are you doing

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:13.140
before you arrest people?

00:21:13.140 --> 00:21:14.840
- And I immediately
thought about the history

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:15.870
of the police too,

00:21:15.870 --> 00:21:18.090
just tracing that back to slave patrols,

00:21:18.090 --> 00:21:21.390
which became our modern
day police departments.

00:21:21.390 --> 00:21:22.860
- Or the fact that like
if you have a felony,

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:23.700
you can't vote.

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:25.170
Like that to me is like

00:21:25.170 --> 00:21:26.490
talk about the loss of the American dream.

00:21:26.490 --> 00:21:28.080
Like you are a citizen of this country.

00:21:28.080 --> 00:21:30.060
You should always have a right to vote.

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:33.483
- [Interviewer] We are
obsessed with race in America.

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:46.710
- I think that word obsessed
is such an interesting word.

00:21:46.710 --> 00:21:49.500
And I think that the way
that that question is framed

00:21:49.500 --> 00:21:51.750
is almost as if it's
like the onus is on us.

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:53.550
Like we are obsessed with race too much.

00:21:53.550 --> 00:21:56.100
Like no white people are obsessed
with race in this country.

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:57.510
They've made their laws based on it.

00:21:57.510 --> 00:21:59.340
They've cast us out because of it.

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:02.340
They've like destroyed
entire groups of people

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:03.173
because of it.

00:22:03.173 --> 00:22:05.340
So I mean, we're just
living in the ripple effects

00:22:05.340 --> 00:22:07.800
of the stones that were
cast in the pawn of history.

00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:09.090
So we're just reacting to it.

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:11.220
So we would rather just be human beings

00:22:11.220 --> 00:22:12.240
than American citizens

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:15.180
and, you know, people that are
pursuing an American dream,

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:16.380
we'd rather not have to think about race,

00:22:16.380 --> 00:22:19.140
but that we did not draw
first blood on that.

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:19.973
- What else do you expect

00:22:19.973 --> 00:22:20.970
given the history of it?
- Yeah, like, I mean,

00:22:20.970 --> 00:22:22.479
I'm not obsessed. (laughs)

00:22:22.479 --> 00:22:23.312
- Yeah.

00:22:23.312 --> 00:22:24.840
- For me being in America

00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:27.390
and give me opportunity to like meet

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:31.290
and like discover so
many different culture

00:22:31.290 --> 00:22:34.290
that I would never see
if I was in my country.

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:36.810
I would just like maybe
hear about it on the news.

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:37.643
Yes, I agree.

00:22:37.643 --> 00:22:40.800
I think they are very obsess with race.

00:22:40.800 --> 00:22:42.780
Even when you do like a easy application,

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:45.000
like it doesn't matter
what application you do,

00:22:45.000 --> 00:22:46.020
they want to know your race.

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:48.030
They want to know, you know,

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:50.040
why they don't have a
category for Africans?

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:51.230
- Exactly.
- You know

00:22:51.230 --> 00:22:52.860
- That's why I'll be
checking out the same time.

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:54.428
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:22:54.428 --> 00:22:55.387
- They wanna know everything.

00:22:55.387 --> 00:22:58.020
- The thing that pissed me off the most is

00:22:58.020 --> 00:22:59.767
that when they see you
and they'll be like,

00:22:59.767 --> 00:23:00.900
"Oh you have an accent.

00:23:00.900 --> 00:23:01.733
Where are you from?"

00:23:01.733 --> 00:23:03.090
Like, who cares where I'm from?

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:05.955
Just sell me this and let me keep going.

00:23:05.955 --> 00:23:07.316
- And when you tell them
that you're African,

00:23:07.316 --> 00:23:08.460
they'll be like, "What country?"

00:23:08.460 --> 00:23:11.593
- Yeah, they're so obsessed
with where you're from.

00:23:11.593 --> 00:23:13.090
- She's like "Wakanda".

00:23:13.090 --> 00:23:16.410
(everyone laughs)

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:19.320
- I don't believe that
there's an obsession

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:20.880
about race in America.

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:24.360
I believe that race is a
distraction to hide the power,

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:26.280
the wealth, and the access to it.

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:29.370
So a lot of times there's
something put on the table,

00:23:29.370 --> 00:23:31.650
like a rock being thrown
and I hold my hand

00:23:31.650 --> 00:23:34.500
and that's the racism
between Black and whites,

00:23:34.500 --> 00:23:35.820
between Blacks and Hispanic

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:38.100
and other cultures who have come here.

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:40.890
We'll talk about race just
so that we don't have access

00:23:40.890 --> 00:23:41.940
to the power and the wealth.

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:43.560
And I think it's done on purpose.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:47.790
There's a power that allows
us to talk about race

00:23:47.790 --> 00:23:49.410
if we don't look past it.

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:52.320
- Some of the question
prompt that I see differently

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:53.827
and then when I hear
somebody say that I'm like,

00:23:53.827 --> 00:23:55.650
"Mm, that actually makes sense

00:23:55.650 --> 00:23:56.850
coming from their perspective."

00:23:56.850 --> 00:23:59.310
So it's been really beautiful
having this conversation

00:23:59.310 --> 00:24:00.143
with you guys.

00:24:00.143 --> 00:24:01.860
You guys are very knowledgeable

00:24:01.860 --> 00:24:04.380
and I've learned a whole lot from each

00:24:04.380 --> 00:24:05.490
and every one of you today.

00:24:05.490 --> 00:24:07.950
- Going into this with like
the title of the series,

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:09.570
like African versus African American,

00:24:09.570 --> 00:24:12.300
I think that like people
were gonna come in thinking

00:24:12.300 --> 00:24:13.740
that we were gonna be like guns blazing,

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:16.080
but this is such a
beautiful display of unity.

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:16.913
I love it.

00:24:16.913 --> 00:24:18.570
It like gave me so much life today.

00:24:18.570 --> 00:24:21.987
(gentle acoustic music)

